{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmMffxE1oFp1Mg4FncNF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, **`gist_space_random_vs_designed_exemplars`**, and the self-check prints `OK`. It does not yet show that the model will fit in time or get through the novelty gate.\n\n**The hypothesis.** People judge randomness by comparing a sequence with remembered examples. Each sequence is placed in a space of three gist features:\n- how often it switches between heads and tails,\n- how lopsided its heads/tails count is,\n- how long its longest streak is.\n\nThere are two kinds of remembered examples:\n- **Random examples:** every sequence of that length, each remembered as often as the person's own picture of a coin would produce it. That picture has a fitted switch rate.\n- **Designed examples:** a streak, perfect alternation, repeated HHT and repeated HHTT, each with its own fitted memorability.\n\nSimilarity falls off exponentially with distance, and the fitted attention weights set how much each feature counts. The chosen sequence is the one whose similarity to random examples most outweighs its similarity to designed ones. People differ in how decisively they act on this, possibly in the reverse direction, and in a habit of favouring one side.\n\n**How it differs from the earlier exemplar models.** Those three lost by 640–900 nats. They compared sequences flip by flip or by rhythm of repeats and switches, and their \"random\" examples were a hand-picked few. This model compares sequences on gist features and remembers every sequence a coin can produce. The longest-streak feature is aimed at the critique's finding that people strongly reward the first breaks of a long streak.\n\n**What the trial fit showed.** In a 300-draw single-chain run there were no divergences. The model put most of its attention on switch rate (0.61), then longest streak (0.31), with little on balance (0.09). The remembered coin switches far more than a fair one (switch rate about 0.83). The smoke fit's ELPD was −3970. That came from a 100-draw fit and is not comparable to the live models (best −3565.5), so this model will very likely rank well below them.\n\n**Speed.** Admission fits use 4 chains of 3,000 draws. The first version, with heavy-tailed person sensitivities, sampled too slowly for the 30-minute limit. Switching them to a Normal population about halved the work per iteration. From the trial run I estimate about 18 minutes per chain, which is under the limit but not by much. The check passed again after the change.\n\nFiles are in `iter_4/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":259,"cache_read_input_tokens":109181,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":259,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"880329b7-d66a-497d-9368-c76c6cc5d5d9","uuid":"ef2d099d-5242-49a5-a186-744414ad294d","timestamp":"2026-10-06T17:12:44.370Z","request_id":"req_011CfmMffaAK4PsBb7YoAk7Q"}
